    "summary": "Legendary Dark Star > St. Stephen > The Eleven with soaring Jerry",
    "blurb": "The second set erupts into a monster Eleven and an epic Dark Star → Saint Stephen segue, highlighted by Jerry’s soaring leads. First‑set material is solid, featuring Friend Of The Devil and Uncle John's Band, while Easy Wind lifts the energy before the show’s abrupt end.",
    "review": "On April 24, 1970 at Mammoth Gardens in Denver, the Dead delivered a performance that has become a touchstone for early‑70s improvisation. While the first set rolls along with tight renditions of Friend Of The Devil, Uncle John's Band and other staples, it’s the second set that truly shines: a soaring Dark Star segues into Saint Stephen and then explodes into an unforgettable Eleven, with Garcia’s tone described as flawless throughout. Easy Wind injects high‑energy momentum before the band dives into a drum‑driven jam and a brief acoustic rendition of It’s A Man’s World, adding a rare intimate moment to the night. Despite the lo‑fi nature of most audience recordings, the show’s musical brilliance shines through, making it a must‑listen for fans seeking that early‑70s magic.",
